---
import Callout from "astro-theme-university/components/Callout.astro";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description="Everybody waits. Not everybody waits equally. Learn who designed the queue, what it measures, and whose time it spends."
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p class="course-subtitle">Twelve Weeks in the Architecture of Waiting</p>

  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <Callout type="info">
    <p>
      Waiting is not simply the absence of service. It is one of the ways a
      system allocates service, risk, attention and priority.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every week adds one artefact to the same file: the SlopU Office of
    Everything, a single fictional counter that queues everything the
    university's central services touch. You measure how it actually behaves,
    choose and defend an ordering rule for it, decide what it can honestly
    promise about how long someone will wait, and finish by redesigning it —
    for normal demand, for congestion, and for the point where it breaks — and
    defending that redesign under questioning.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it is worth taking</h2>
  <p>
    Most courses that touch queues teach the mathematics of them. This one
    teaches the argument: a queue is not a delay that happens to a system, it
    is a decision someone made about whose time matters more this week. Once
    you can see that decision, you cannot stop seeing it — in call centres,
    hospital triage, ticket support, exam scheduling, anywhere a line forms.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone comfortable reading a small dataset without being taught what a
    spreadsheet is. No queueing theory, statistics or systems-design
    background is assumed — the course builds its vocabulary from one running
    case, not a survey of the literature. It particularly suits anyone who has
    ever been annoyed by a badly designed waiting room, call centre or online
    queue and wanted the words to say exactly what was wrong with it.
  </p>

  <h2>By week 12</h2>
  <p>
    You can take an unfamiliar waiting system, name what it is actually
    measuring, identify the policy choice behind its ordering rule, say who
    that choice favours, and propose a specific change to it — including what
    the change would cost someone else.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>What is covered, week by week, and which topics each lecture draws on.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly exercise against the Office of Everything's own records.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>Rules for late work, extensions, sources and submitted prototypes.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-subtitle {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
